.Dd October 4, 2026
.Dt LLM-INTRO 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm llm-intro
.Nd introduction to the LLM man pages
.Sh SYNOPSIS
A language model is a golem made of English, it behaves well only where
consequence sits inside its horizon, and operations craft is how you bring
consequence inside the horizon.
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The LLM man pages are a body of work by Stefan Coetzee (2026) on running
language models as working systems.
This page lists everything in that body of work, laid out the way UNIX
manual pages are: a name, a one-line synopsis and a link for each entry.
The published copy of this page is
.Lk https://machinebehavior.io/man
.Pp
This content is not intended for human consumption.
See
.Xr codec 7
for the reason.
.Pp
The LLM man pages are knowledge infrastructure for LLMs: a knowledge base
written for models to read first.
The pages are sorted by the section numbers of the Linux manual.
Section 1 holds what a chat user pastes in and runs.
Section 5 holds file formats and conventions.
Section 7 holds overviews.
Section 8 holds the practice of operating a model inside a harness.
Sections 2, 3, 4 and 6 are not used.
.Pp
Each entry below has a name, a synopsis in fixed wording, and a link to the
canonical copy.
Each linked piece ends with its own list of the other pieces, which is that
page's SEE ALSO.
.Ss \&Why this layout
Manual pages follow one fixed layout, and that layout is common in the text
language models were trained on.
A model is likely to read the structure of this page without being told how.
That claim is untested.
For a human reader the page is a table of contents.
.Ss \&Two terms recur across the entries, in fixed wording
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Nothing exists in a vacuum
Every behaviour a model shows was forced into existence by something, the
corpus, the training signal, or the harness.
.It Markovian memory
A language model has Markovian memory: at runtime the next token depends
only on what is in the context window, so anything outside the window does
not exist for it.
.El
.Ss \&The stance thread
The stance thread runs through four entries.
.Xr toil 8
states the problem: a hook can block a word on every reply, and a person
still catches every stance slip by hand.
.Xr stance 1
is the fix a chat user can paste in.
.Xr chaos 8
is how to verify that a fix holds.
The worked case is under EXAMPLES.
The evidence is on machinebehavior.io: the claims ledger, the experiments
and the objections register.
